Hi Paul and everybody,

Le Thu, Mar 12, 2026 at 07:25:58AM +0100, Paul Gevers a écrit :
> r-base is a key package (some r-cran packages are as well by the way,
> by the looks via r-recommended), it's extra work (typically not
> trivial) to remove it. It probably requires also changes to how some
> of our desktop environment manage their dependencies if it wouldn't
> force them out completely on those architectures. Please contact the
> porters before you do that.

https://release.debian.org/key-packages.html states:

	Key packages, what are they? In order to run a (minimal) Debian
	system, there is a set of package that need to be available in a
	suite."

I know about key packages and I just want to forward here to the readers
of his list what was said on the debian-release last year.

In https://lists.debian.org/debian-release/2025/11/msg00859.html, Chris
wrote:

	r-% in the key set are:
	  r-base
	  r-cran-class
	  r-cran-mass
	  r-cran-nnet
	  r-cran-spatial

	One chain is:

	meta:d-i	=>	debian-edu-install
	debian-edu-install	depends	debian-edu-config
	debian-edu-config	depends	debian-edu
	debian-edu	depends	tasksel
	tasksel	depends	meta-kde
	meta-kde	depends	cantor
	cantor	build-depends	r-base

	r-base	depends	r-cran-class
	r-base	depends	r-cran-mass
	r-base	depends	r-cran-nnet
	r-base	depends	r-cran-spatial

And Holger added in https://lists.debian.org/debian-release/2025/11/msg00867.html 

	Debian Edu is basically an amd64 blend.

to which Chris answered:

	Probably just one of many things the key package set does not and
	maybe should not take into account.

	Incidentally I looked into either the calculation or the autoremoval
	script today and noticed that "cantor" is marked as allow-remove.
	Maybe the r-* packages do not matter for the key package set, in the
	end.

This is why it may look like I do not know about key packages…

Have a nice week-end,

Charles

